WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.490
BUKOLA AYOCELE: So far, we've
covered three strategies

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.740
for scaling your
serverless apps.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.039
Lucky for Critter Junction, we
have three more up our sleeves.

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:08.700
Stay tuned.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.389
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.040
To review, Critter
Junction's new wallet app

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.340
lets users send and
receive virtual currency.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.520
They're using Cloud Functions
to provide response to queries

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.590
around wallet amounts.

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:30.270
But they hit their database
back end rate limit,

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.750
so they couldn't experience
the scale that serverless

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.420
provides, and wondered what
other tips they were missing.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.260
So my team came in and helped
them set up instant scaling

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.930
limits on Cloud Functions,
Cloud Tasks, to limit

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.360
the rate of work done,
and stateful storage

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.240
to defer results from
long running operations.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.670
Let's explore three more tips.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.970
#4, use Redis to
rate limit requests.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.620
Sometimes you need
to perform rate

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.190
limiting in the context
of the http request.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.740
For example, you might
want to protect your system

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.770
from abusive users with
per user rate limits,

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.960
like 100 wallet
queries per minute,

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.770
to control the flow of requests
your serverless workloads.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.540
Or you might need
a back pressure

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.730
to the caller of your
serverless workload,

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.900
like if your wallet
transactions called

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.730
the API that check for fraud,
which has a limit of 100

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.230
requests per second.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:24.050
You need a defense measure
to ensure all your serverless

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.090
functions respect
that limit and prevent

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.460
the API from excessive use.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.050
And Redis is great
for doing this.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.610
It gives you a very low
latency key value datastore,

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.100
plus basic data
structures and operations

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.230
that make it one of
the preferred backing

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.390
source for rate limiting.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.000
Google Cloud offers a
fully managed Redis service

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.160
called Cloud Memory Store.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.880
If you want to learn how to use
Redis to limit cost function

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:53.240
invocations, check
out the link below.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:53.990
Tip 5.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:58.040
Use Cloud Pub/Sub up to
process the work in batches.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.380
When you're dealing with a
large number of messages,

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:03.410
you might not want to
process every message.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.870
A common pattern is
to wait until messages

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:08.330
have accumulated.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:11.510
Cloud Functions integrate with
Cloud Pub/Sub in messaging

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.250
and ingestion middleware.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.640
Instead of invoking a Cloud
Function for every message,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.940
serverless workloads can
use Pub/Sub as a place

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.060
to accumulate batches of work,
since the service will store

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.400
messages for up to seven days.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.760
Then you can use Cloud Scheduler
in managed cron job service

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.460
to process these
items on schedule.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.620
You can even trigger
the batch processes

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.130
based on the number and age
of accumulated messages.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:38.720
This will help you
control the flow,

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.210
and your functions can
better handle increases

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:43.070
in trigger requests.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.410
And finally, our last tip.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:49.040
Use Cloud Run for
heavily I/O work.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.770
One of the most expensive
components of infrastructure

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.540
is compute cycle.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.370
When your serverless
workload is just

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:59.000
waiting for a remote API call
or waiting for files to read,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.130
these are billable moments.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.590
Cloud Run, a managed
platform for running

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:06.110
stateless containers,
lets you specify

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.990
how many concurrent
requests, it can handle.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.450
This can mean big
increases in efficiency

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.610
for I/O bound workloads.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.040
For example, if
the work being done

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.560
spends most of its time
waiting for replies

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.190
from slow remote
API calls, Cloud Run

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:24.320
supports up to 80
concurrent requests

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.580
on the same instance, which
shares the same CPU allocation.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.880
What you want to do, is one,
tune your service performance

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.340
for the maximum
stable concurrency.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.760
Two, set your expected
level of concurrency

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.450
in any code level configuration
and deploy your service.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:45.080
Three, set Cloud Run
concurrency equal to or less

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.750
than any code level
configuration.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:50.480
Four, then use low
testing tools that

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.790
support a configurable
concurrency,

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:56.150
and make sure that your
service remains stable.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:58.280
Critter Junction now
has six strategies

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.310
for their serverless scaling
in their back pocket.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.400
They can use rate limiting,
set instance scaling limits,

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.910
and products like Cloud Run,
Cloud Task, Cloud Scheduler

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:10.340
and Cloud Pub/Sub
to help manage load

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.620
on their serverless instance.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.260
Even highly scalable
systems should have limits

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:18.680
on consumption to reduce the
chance of cascading failures

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.180
and maximize throughput.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:23.750
For more details on these tips
and an awesome decision tree,

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:25.370
check out the link below.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.140
That's a wrap for a
"Season of Scale."

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:30.770
Stay tuned for more strategies
for scaling on Google Cloud.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.360
And remember, always
be architecting.

